<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roman triumph</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Roman triumph was a ceremonial procession through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> granted to a victorious general, in which he paraded with his troops, captives and spoils. Under the emperors the honour was increasingly reserved for members of the imperial family. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantian Empire</a> note that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-cornelius-balbus">Lucius Cornelius Balbus</a> received the last triumph awarded to a non-imperial general, for his campaign against the Garamantes in 19 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roman-triumph.jpg" alt="Roman triumph" width="285" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wilfredor · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bas_relief_from_Arch_of_Marcus_Aurelius_triumph_chariot_from_Roma_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Roman historians placed the first triumph in the mythical past. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> and some Roman sources traced it, and the kingly garb of the triumphator, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s first king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a>, whose defeat of King Acron of the Caeninenses was thought to date from the foundation of Rome in 753 BCE. Roman etymologists thought the soldiers' chant of triumpe came through Etruscan from the Greek thriambus, cried out in Dionysian processions.</p><p>Like much in Roman culture, the triumph drew on Etruscan and Greek precedents. The purple, embroidered toga picta of the general was thought to derive from the royal toga of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s Etruscan kings. The earliest triumphs were probably simple victory parades, ending with some dedication to the gods.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>On the day, the general wore a laurel crown, the toga picta and red boots, regalia tied to the monarchy and to the statue of Jupiter Capitolinus. In some accounts his face was painted red. He rode in a four-horse <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> in unarmed procession, with spoils and captives leading the way and his army behind. At the temple of Capitoline Jupiter he sacrificed two white oxen and laid tokens of victory at the statue's feet.</p><p>The procession gathered on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campus-martius">Campus Martius</a>, entered the city through a Porta Triumphalis, passed through the Forum along the Via Sacra and climbed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitoline Hill</a>, a distance of just under 4 km. Triumphs were notoriously long and slow, and the longest lasted two or three days. Banquets and games, usually paid for by the general from his share of the loot, followed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In Republican tradition only the Senate could grant a triumph, after a debate and vote confirmed by a people's assembly. No firm evidence shows fixed triumphal laws, although Valerius Maximus listed some, such as a requirement to have killed 5,000 enemies in a single battle. Some triumphs went ahead after the Senate refused, and a lesser honour, the ovation, was granted to Marcus Marcellus in 211 BCE and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a> in 71 BCE.</p><p>In the Late Republic triumphs grew more frequent and lavish. Triumphal generals minted coins to spread their fame, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> did. Under Augustus the honour became an imperial privilege. The last triumph on the Fasti Triumphales is for 19 BCE, and others received only triumphal ornaments or an ovation.</p><p>Later rulers imitated the triumph. In 1325 the city of Lucca gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castruccio-castracani">Castruccio Castracani</a> a Roman-style triumph after the Battle of Altopascio. Petrarch's Triumphs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-mantegna">Andrea Mantegna</a>'s Triumphs of Caesar (1484-92) and Maximilian I's Triumphal Procession (1512-19) drew on it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>'s royal entry into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 5 April 1536 followed the ancient route and was described as a triumph over the infidel.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Roman triumph” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
